# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

## Goal
Produce a sample PCA from the gene counts, with one plot coloured by treated versus untreated.

## Inputs
Artifact types are resolved again at execution time using `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`. Paths below are relative to the project and reflect the current resolution; inputs remain read-only.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. Execute Python in the existing `gars-bio` environment, sourced through `_system/gars-env.sh`, using pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and matplotlib (noninteractive Agg backend). Install nothing.
2. Read the resolved count TSV and design CSV. Use `gene_id` as the feature identifier, exclude the annotation column `gene_name`, and align sample columns exactly to `sample_id` in the design. Require unique gene and sample identifiers, a one-to-one sample match, finite nonnegative numeric counts, positive library totals, and condition values restricted to `treated` and `untreated` with both represented. Stop on validation failure. The current design contains six samples: A1–A3 untreated and B1–B3 treated. Do not exclude samples.
3. Remove genes with zero counts across all samples. Normalize each sample to counts per million using its total over all input genes, then transform as `log2(CPM + 1)`. Preserve fractional counts without rounding. Retain all genes with nonzero variance after transformation; do not select features by condition or perform differential expression or batch correction.
4. Transpose to samples by genes and run `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=2, svd_solver="full")`. Center each gene across samples; do not scale genes to unit variance. Require at least two usable features and two nonzero principal components; otherwise stop with the reason.
5. Create exactly one plot: PC1 versus PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition` (untreated: blue `#0072B2`; treated: orange `#D55E00`). Label points by sample ID, include a condition legend, and label axes with explained variance percentages. Save as `results/sample_pca.png` at 300 dpi, 7 by 5 inches. Describe sample variation only; make no treatment-effect or significance claims.

